BEGIN;
DO $$
DECLARE
  changes jsonb := '{"ricp-prep":{"additions":{"module-1":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-1\">Model cash flow over time and test the order of returns</a> · <a href=\"#check-1\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-2":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-2\">Compare claiming options with a survivor budget</a> · <a href=\"#check-2\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-3":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-3\">Design a distribution and tax calendar</a> · <a href=\"#check-3\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-4":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-4\">Turn a retirement gap into a savings target</a> · <a href=\"#check-4\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","capstone":"<h4>Second case</h4><p><a href=\"#advanced-case\">The early retirement bridge</a></p>","start":"<h4>Study sequence</h4><p>In each module, begin with the foundation lessons, then work through the added decision lesson and application check. Write your reasoning before opening the answer. Finish both cases and the course review. The module checks are self-scored.</p><p><a href=\"#capstone\">Start the capstones</a> · <a href=\"https://www.theamericancollege.edu/learn/professional-designations-certifications/ricp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Issuer’s curriculum</a> · <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Retirement distributions and beneficiary rules</a></p>"},"byModule":{"module-1":[{"id":"deep-1","title":"Model cash flow over time and test the order of returns","html":"<h3>Model cash flow over time and test the order of returns</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Model beginning balance, annual withdrawals and returns separately. Withdrawals make early declines especially costly because less principal participates in the recovery. Compare identical returns in different orders. Specify whether withdrawals happen before or after growth and distinguish nominal spending from purchasing power.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>With $500,000 and a $25,000 year-end draw, returns of −20% then +25% give end balances $375,000 and $443,750. Reversing returns gives $600,000 then $455,000. Without withdrawals, both orders end at $500,000. The difference is path risk.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-1\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-1\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-1","title":"Module 1 application check","html":"<h3>Module 1 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>Use $30,000 year-end withdrawals and the same returns. Calculate both results and explain what a cash reserve could change.</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$430,000 and $430,000</li><li>$450,000 and $432,500</li><li>$470,000 and $470,000</li><li>Bad-first ends at $432,500</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>D. Bad-first ends at $432,500. Good-first ends at $446,000. A reserve may reduce forced sales during a downturn, but cash has an opportunity cost and must be funded and replenished.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-1\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-2":[{"id":"deep-2","title":"Compare claiming options with a survivor budget","html":"<h3>Compare claiming options with a survivor budget</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Map each reliable income source to its start, end, inflation adjustment and survivor treatment. Build the bridge separately from ongoing spending. Check official benefit estimates and compare household cash flow after the first death. Delaying benefits may increase reliable income later while requiring more portfolio spending earlier.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>A hypothetical household spends $84,000. Plan A pays $45,000 from year one. Plan B pays $27,000 for three years then $54,000. Plan B requires $54,000 more bridge withdrawals and reduces later annual draw by $9,000. The crude recovery takes six years after the bridge, before returns, taxes or survivor effects.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-2\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-2\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-2","title":"Module 2 application check","html":"<h3>Module 2 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>If the bridge lasts two years instead, how many years of $9,000 annual savings recover the extra withdrawals? List two omitted variables.</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Six years</li><li>Eight years</li><li>Four years</li><li>Two years</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>C. Four years. The extra $18,000 annual draw lasts two years. Verify tax, survivor payments, longevity, inflation and investment returns.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-2\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-3":[{"id":"deep-3","title":"Design a distribution and tax calendar","html":"<h3>Design a distribution and tax calendar</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>List withdrawal sources and verify current-year minimum distributions and rollover eligibility before moving funds. Separate cash need from taxable income created. Compare taking money from taxable, pretax and Roth sources and the effect on future balances, taxes and benefit thresholds. Plan payments and timing with tax support.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>A household needs $50,000 net. A simplified pretax withdrawal taxed at 20% requires $62,500 gross. If $20,000 net is instead funded from available cash without added tax, the remaining $30,000 requires $37,500 gross. The household uses $20,000 cash and pays $7,500 modeled tax.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-3\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-3\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-3","title":"Module 3 application check","html":"<h3>Module 3 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>At a hypothetical 25% rate, how much gross pretax withdrawal is needed for $30,000 net? What else matters before selecting the withdrawal source?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$45,000</li><li>$40,000</li><li>$37,500</li><li>$32,500</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>B. $40,000. Divide net by 0.75. Verify actual marginal effects, required distributions, basis, how much cash remains and future tax flexibility.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-3\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-4":[{"id":"deep-4","title":"Turn a retirement gap into a savings target","html":"<h3>Turn a retirement gap into a savings target</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Set a target in consistent dollars, project existing investments, then solve for the shortfall and periodic savings. Confirm employer match and its vesting and avoid treating a forecast as certain. Compare a later retirement date, lower spending or more saving instead of increasing the assumed return to make the plan work.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>A worker has $300,000, 10 years to retire and assumes 3% real growth. Existing assets project to $403,175. A $550,000 target leaves about $146,825 to fund. Annual end-of-year savings earn a 10-year annuity factor of about 11.464, requiring roughly $12,808 per year.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-4\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-4\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-4","title":"Module 4 application check","html":"<h3>Module 4 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>If your target is $500,000 instead, what annual real savings is needed? What changes for beginning-of-year savings?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>About $8,446 at year-end</li><li>$9,683</li><li>$50,000</li><li>$12,808</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>A. About $8,446 at year-end. Earlier deposits receive one extra year of growth, so required annual savings fall.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-4\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"capstone":[{"id":"advanced-case","title":"The early retirement bridge","html":"<h3>The early retirement bridge</h3><h4>Case facts</h4><p>A couple has $1.1 million of investments and needs $88,000 a year. Reliable income is $32,000 in the next three years and $64,000 thereafter. A care event increases annual spending by $14,000 starting in year four. One scenario assumes a 15% early investment decline.</p><h4>Submit a written recommendation</h4><p>Build base, care and market-shock cases. Compare bridging with cash versus portfolio sales and name two facts you need to verify.</p><details><summary>Reveal the worked case and compare your reasoning</summary><p>Base bridge is $56,000 per year and $168,000 over three years before growth. Steady draw is $24,000 or $38,000 with care. A 15% shock reduces $1.1 million to $935,000, putting the first draw at about 5.99%. Do not apply that balance to year four without projecting the intervening withdrawals. Verify survivor income and coverage for care.</p></details><h4>Review your recommendation</h4><ol><li>Reconcile each calculation to source facts.</li><li>Identify which results depend on assumptions or documents.</li><li>Explain one alternative, the trade-off, and the next action.</li></ol>"}]}},"clu-prep":{"additions":{"module-1":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-1\">Set a coverage budget around multiple risks</a> · <a href=\"#check-1\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-2":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-2\">Compare permanent-life funding scenarios</a> · <a href=\"#check-2\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-3":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-3\">Trace insurance through an estate plan</a> · <a href=\"#check-3\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-4":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-4\">Fund the obligation named in the buy-sell agreement</a> · <a href=\"#check-4\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-5":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-5\">Measure surrender cash separately from taxable proceeds</a> · <a href=\"#check-5\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-6":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-6\">Document a recommendation when coverage competes with liquidity</a> · <a href=\"#check-6\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","capstone":"<h4>Second case</h4><p><a href=\"#advanced-case\">The second-generation buyout</a></p>","start":"<h4>Study sequence</h4><p>In each module, begin with the foundation lessons, then work through the added decision lesson and application check. Write your reasoning before opening the answer. Finish both cases and the course review. The module checks are self-scored.</p><p><a href=\"#capstone\">Start the capstones</a> · <a href=\"https://www.theamericancollege.edu/learn/professional-designations-certifications/clu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Issuer’s curriculum</a> · <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">IRS income and insurance proceeds reference</a></p>"},"byModule":{"module-1":[{"id":"deep-1","title":"Set a coverage budget around multiple risks","html":"<h3>Set a coverage budget around multiple risks</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Quantify retained cost and income interruption before comparing contracts. Map health, disability, property and liability exposures separately, then model simultaneous losses. Prioritize exposures a household cannot absorb. Verify whether employer benefits replace gross or net income and whether waiting periods match reserves.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>A family has $24,000 in cash. Essential spending is $5,000 monthly, and income would stop for a 90-day disability waiting period. A separate covered health claim requires $6,000 out of pocket. The initial combined cash need is $21,000 before any other costs.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-1\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-1\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-1","title":"Module 1 application check","html":"<h3>Module 1 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>If other household income provides $1,500 monthly during the wait, what remains of the reserve after both costs?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$3,000</li><li>$13,500</li><li>$16,500</li><li>$7,500</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>D. $7,500. The spending gap is $3,500 × 3 and the health cost is $6,000, totaling $16,500.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-1\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-2":[{"id":"deep-2","title":"Compare permanent-life funding scenarios","html":"<h3>Compare permanent-life funding scenarios</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Read the policy’s guarantees, charges, surrender schedule and assumptions. Obtain current and guaranteed in-force ledgers. Distinguish net cash surrender value from death benefit and check how loans, withdrawals and premium changes affect each. For proposed replacement, compare underwriting and timing before terminating any in-force protection.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>An in-force policy has $68,000 gross cash value, $15,000 loan and $4,000 surrender charge. Simplified net surrender proceeds are $49,000 before tax. If stated policy value grows $3,000 but the loan grows $1,500 and the charge is unchanged, net proceeds grow only $1,500.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-2\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-2\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-2","title":"Module 2 application check","html":"<h3>Module 2 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>What are the updated net proceeds? What should you ask the carrier if keeping the policy?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$57,000</li><li>$71,000</li><li>$50,500</li><li>$53,000</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>C. $50,500. Ask for current and guaranteed projections under the actual premium and loan schedule, including projected lapse and benefit changes.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-2\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-3":[{"id":"deep-3","title":"Trace insurance through an estate plan","html":"<h3>Trace insurance through an estate plan</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>For each policy identify incidents of control, payee, access timing and contractual obligations. Distinguish estate inclusion from the path a benefit takes at death. Coordinate document execution and tax assumptions with counsel. Build estate funding apart from cash available to named family beneficiaries.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>An estate needs $210,000 immediately. It holds $75,000 cash and a business interest that cannot be sold promptly. One policy pays $100,000 to the estate and another pays $400,000 to children. Under those assumptions the modeled estate cash gap is $35,000.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-3\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-3\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-3","title":"Module 3 application check","html":"<h3>Module 3 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>What is the modeled gap if the $100,000 policy is also payable to the children?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$210,000</li><li>$135,000</li><li>$35,000</li><li>$0</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>B. $135,000. Only the $75,000 estate cash is identified as available to meet those bills.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-3\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-4":[{"id":"deep-4","title":"Fund the obligation named in the buy-sell agreement","html":"<h3>Fund the obligation named in the buy-sell agreement</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Review death, disability and voluntary departure triggers. Identify the legal buyer, current valuation, payment timing and eligible funding source. Separate key-person liquidity from purchase funding. Have attorney and tax advisors compare ownership structures and the treatment of corporate insurance proceeds before implementation.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>Three equal shareholders use an agreed $4.5 million valuation and one dies. The buyer owes $1.5 million. A policy payable to that buyer provides $1.1 million, leaving $400,000. A second policy payable to the company for continuity is a different resource unless counsel confirms a valid use for the purchase.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-4\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-4\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-4","title":"Module 4 application check","html":"<h3>Module 4 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>If the agreement instead requires an initial $900,000 payment and $600,000 later, and the buyer has $1.1 million in available proceeds, how much remains for the later obligation after initial payment?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$200,000 remaining proceeds, so $400,000 still needs another source for the later installment.</li><li>$600,000 remaining proceeds</li><li>$1.1 million remaining</li><li>$400,000 remaining proceeds</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>A. $200,000 remaining proceeds, so $400,000 still needs another source for the later installment.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-4\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-5":[{"id":"deep-5","title":"Measure surrender cash separately from taxable proceeds","html":"<h3>Measure surrender cash separately from taxable proceeds</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>A surrender analysis needs the carrier’s confirmed values, investment in the contract, loan balance and how debt is treated. A loan can reduce cash received while affecting the amount considered distributed. Account for these differences before modeling tax. Check modified endowment contract and replacement considerations separately.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>Assume a carrier reports $82,000 as the total amount distributed for tax analysis, including $20,000 of loan discharge. Cash actually received is $62,000. With verified $65,000 investment in the contract, simplified gain is $17,000 under the exercise assumptions.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-5\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-5\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-5","title":"Module 5 application check","html":"<h3>Module 5 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>Why is subtracting the $65,000 basis from the $62,000 cash check the wrong calculation here?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Loans always erase taxable gain</li><li>The check is always the full amount distributed</li><li>Basis is ignored on surrender</li><li>The exercise gives $82,000 as the total amount distributed for tax analysis</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>D. The exercise gives $82,000 as the total amount distributed for tax analysis. Use that amount and verify the carrier’s reporting with the tax advisor.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-5\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-6":[{"id":"deep-6","title":"Document a recommendation when coverage competes with liquidity","html":"<h3>Document a recommendation when coverage competes with liquidity</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Set the client’s stated loss exposure, household time horizon and reserve needs. Compare insurance options using a consistent benefit amount and clearly show differences in permanence, guarantees and future insurability. Assess whether the proposed premium would crowd out urgent savings or create lapse risk. Record costs and conflicts before the client chooses.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>A client can commit $850 a month and needs $500,000 of coverage for 20 years. A term quote is $190 and a permanent quote $760. The remaining monthly budget differs by $570, before any investment assumptions. A permanent benefit might meet a lasting obligation, but first document whether such a goal exists.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-6\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-6\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-6","title":"Module 6 application check","html":"<h3>Module 6 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>How much more annual cash is available under the term quote using the given figures?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$9,120</li><li>$10,200</li><li>$6,840</li><li>$570</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>C. $6,840. Multiply $570 by 12.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-6\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"capstone":[{"id":"advanced-case","title":"The second-generation buyout","html":"<h3>The second-generation buyout</h3><h4>Case facts</h4><p>Three owners hold equal shares in a company with a $3.6 million agreed equity value. A buyer owns $950,000 of death benefit on one owner. A separate $300,000 company-owned policy is meant for operating losses. The deceased owner’s spouse expects $420,000 of income replacement and has $180,000 of personal coverage.</p><h4>Submit a written recommendation</h4><p>Trace all three benefit paths. Estimate the buyout and household gaps, then assign a valuation, document and underwriting review.</p><details><summary>Reveal the worked case and compare your reasoning</summary><p>Initial buyout is $1.2 million, leaving $250,000 after buyer-owned proceeds. Household gap is $240,000. Treat the company-owned policy separately unless reviewed arrangements legally make it available. Check trigger, payout timetable, whose policy pays whom and whether valuation remains current.</p></details><h4>Review your recommendation</h4><ol><li>Reconcile each calculation to source facts.</li><li>Identify which results depend on assumptions or documents.</li><li>Explain one alternative, the trade-off, and the next action.</li></ol>"}]}},"cic-prep":{"additions":{"module-1":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-1\">Read the policy in the order a claim requires</a> · <a href=\"#check-1\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-2":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-2\">Estimate a covered shutdown with a business-income worksheet</a> · <a href=\"#check-2\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-3":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-3\">Compare occurrence and claims-made triggers</a> · <a href=\"#check-3\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-4":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-4\">Layer excess insurance over the actual underlying risk</a> · <a href=\"#check-4\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-5":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-5\">Test protection when employment ends</a> · <a href=\"#check-5\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-6":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-6\">Run a renewal with an auditable coverage decision</a> · <a href=\"#check-6\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-7":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-7\">Read insurer results and recognize risk transfer</a> · <a href=\"#check-7\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","capstone":"<h4>Second case</h4><p><a href=\"#advanced-case\">The manufacturer’s two-loss renewal</a></p>","start":"<h4>Study sequence</h4><p>In each module, begin with the foundation lessons, then work through the added decision lesson and application check. Write your reasoning before opening the answer. Finish both cases and the course review. The module checks are self-scored.</p><p><a href=\"#capstone\">Start the capstones</a> · <a href=\"https://www.riskeducation.org/programs/cic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Issuer’s curriculum</a> · <a href=\"https://tdi.texas.gov/pubs/consumer/cb021.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TDI commercial property and business coverage guide</a></p>"},"byModule":{"module-1":[{"id":"deep-1","title":"Read the policy in the order a claim requires","html":"<h3>Read the policy in the order a claim requires</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Start with the declarations to locate the insured, period and limits. Read the coverage grant, definitions, exclusions, conditions and endorsements together. Track which part of the loss damaged the insured’s property and which created liability to another person. Separate water source, entry path and any resulting damage. Record facts that could change the answer.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>A storm causes wind damage to the roof, surface water enters a garage and an injured guest makes a claim. Open separate questions for dwelling damage, flood and liability. A wind endorsement, a flood policy and a liability exclusion can direct different outcomes. Request dates, photos, cause evidence and current forms before quoting a settlement.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-1\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-1\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-1","title":"Module 1 application check","html":"<h3>Module 1 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>Which homeowner declaration is sufficient on its own to decide whether surface-water damage is covered?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Dwelling limit</li><li>Deductible amount</li><li>Mortgage company name</li><li>None</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>D. None. Read the coverage grant, applicable water exclusions and endorsements and check any separate flood policy.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-1\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-2":[{"id":"deep-2","title":"Estimate a covered shutdown with a business-income worksheet","html":"<h3>Estimate a covered shutdown with a business-income worksheet</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Forecast the operations that would have occurred without the loss. For the restoration period, estimate lost net income and qualifying continuing expenses. Separate saved expenses and eligible extra expense. Check the waiting period, restoration definition, limits, reporting basis and dependent-property wording. A location’s insurance value and business-income exposure answer different questions.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>A café would have earned $120,000 revenue and incurred $105,000 expenses over the stated period. During closure $38,000 of expenses continue. The simplified business-income estimate is $15,000 net income plus $38,000, or $53,000, before policy terms. A $14,000 temporary-kitchen charge needs its own coverage review.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-2\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-2\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-2","title":"Module 2 application check","html":"<h3>Module 2 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>If qualifying continuing expenses are $28,000 instead, what is the simplified estimate?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$53,000</li><li>$120,000</li><li>$43,000 before limits and adjustments.</li><li>$28,000</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>C. $43,000 before limits and adjustments.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-2\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-3":[{"id":"deep-3","title":"Compare occurrence and claims-made triggers","html":"<h3>Compare occurrence and claims-made triggers</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>For occurrence coverage, locate when injury or damage took place as defined. For claims-made coverage, identify when a claim was first made, the applicable retroactive date and whether reporting meets the form. An extended reporting period can affect reporting deadlines under its terms. Check prior acts, exclusions and aggregation separately.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>Assume a claims-made-and-reported policy runs through December and has a January 2023 retroactive date. Alleged work occurred in 2024. A claim is first made and reported the following January after expiration. Even though the work follows the retroactive date, regular reporting is outside the stated term. Check renewal or extended reporting provisions before concluding.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-3\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-3\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-3","title":"Module 3 application check","html":"<h3>Module 3 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>Which dates need review in the example?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only the year the agent renewed their license</li><li>The alleged act, retroactive date, first claim, report and policy or extended reporting periods.</li><li>Only the act date</li><li>Only invoice date</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>B. The alleged act, retroactive date, first claim, report and policy or extended reporting periods.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-3\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-4":[{"id":"deep-4","title":"Layer excess insurance over the actual underlying risk","html":"<h3>Layer excess insurance over the actual underlying risk</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>List coverage, limits and exclusions at each layer. An excess policy may add limits to scheduled underlying coverage, while an umbrella may have its own coverage terms. Check schedules, exhaustion, exclusions and retained limits. For cyber or crime, identify the transfer mechanism and compare first-party losses with liability claims.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>Assume $1 million primary liability and $4 million excess both cover the same occurrence. A covered $1.65 million loss exhausts primary and uses $650,000 of excess. If the excess excludes that exposure, the result changes even if the schedule shows the primary policy.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-4\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-4\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-4","title":"Module 4 application check","html":"<h3>Module 4 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>If both policies respond and the covered loss is $5.4 million, what is the amount above the combined stated limits?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$400,000, before other coverage or adjustments.</li><li>$650,000</li><li>$1 million</li><li>$5 million</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>A. $400,000, before other coverage or adjustments.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-4\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-5":[{"id":"deep-5","title":"Test protection when employment ends","html":"<h3>Test protection when employment ends</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Build separate timelines for disability, death, medical eligibility and care. Compare group contracts with individual coverage and verify conversion and portability rights before relying on them. Use after-tax monthly amounts for disability cash flow and account for the waiting period.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>A family needs $5,600 monthly. Net group disability is $3,200 after a two-month waiting period. Immediate reserve need is $11,200 if no other income, and the ongoing gap is $2,400. Loss of the job could remove group coverage depending on terms, so model that separately.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-5\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-5\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-5","title":"Module 5 application check","html":"<h3>Module 5 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>What is the combined need for the two-month wait and two later months with the stated benefit?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$11,200</li><li>$13,600</li><li>$22,400</li><li>$16,000</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>D. $16,000. Add $11,200 and $4,800.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-5\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-6":[{"id":"deep-6","title":"Run a renewal with an auditable coverage decision","html":"<h3>Run a renewal with an auditable coverage decision</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Build the file from exposure discovery, market submission and quote comparison through authorization, binding and delivery. Confirm the actual policy matches the bound terms. Record declined coverage and unresolved exceptions with owners and dates. Certificates summarize, while the policy and endorsements govern rights.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>A contractor renews with a carrier that excludes the largest subcontracted operation. The new premium is lower. Compare quotations by class, operations and exclusions, explain the uncovered scenario and document the client''s choice before binding. Verify issued forms after delivery.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-6\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-6\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-6","title":"Module 6 application check","html":"<h3>Module 6 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>What evidence best confirms additional insured rights under a new policy?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A quote cover page</li><li>A prior term’s endorsement automatically</li><li>An applicable endorsement or actual policy wording that grants the rights under the stated facts.</li><li>A certificate request email alone</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>C. An applicable endorsement or actual policy wording that grants the rights under the stated facts.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-6\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-7":[{"id":"deep-7","title":"Read insurer results and recognize risk transfer","html":"<h3>Read insurer results and recognize risk transfer</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Separate written from earned premiums, paid from incurred losses and acquisition from operating costs. A combined ratio compares underwriting costs on stated accounting bases. For a simplified consistent basis, add loss and expense ratios. Reinsurance can limit insurer exposure, with terms governing attachment, limits and credit risk. Review reserve development when comparing periods.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>On a consistent illustrative basis, $12 million earned premium, $8.4 million incurred losses and $3 million expenses yield 70% plus 25%, or 95%. A $1.2 million adverse loss development raises the result to 105% before investment income.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-7\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-7\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-7","title":"Module 7 application check","html":"<h3>Module 7 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>If loss development is $600,000 instead, what is the simplified combined ratio?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>105%</li><li>100%</li><li>95%</li><li>97%</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>B. 100%. Loss ratio becomes 75%, plus 25% expenses.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-7\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"capstone":[{"id":"advanced-case","title":"The manufacturer’s two-loss renewal","html":"<h3>The manufacturer’s two-loss renewal</h3><h4>Case facts</h4><p>A factory worth $2 million carries $1.2 million at an 80% coinsurance requirement. An assumed covered partial loss is $240,000 before a $10,000 deductible. Separately, a customer alleges $700,000 of harm, with an occurrence limit of $1 million and $450,000 left in the applicable aggregate.</p><h4>Submit a written recommendation</h4><p>Estimate both stated limits, list contract and timeline issues to verify, and document one renewal option to address each gap.</p><details><summary>Reveal the worked case and compare your reasoning</summary><p>Property requires $1.6 million and the insured carried 75% of that, so the simplified payment is $170,000. Liability has up to $450,000 under the remaining aggregate if the claim is covered. Verify forms, time period, exclusions and remaining limits. Consider insurance-to-value review and liability or excess options under their actual terms.</p></details><h4>Review your recommendation</h4><ol><li>Reconcile each calculation to source facts.</li><li>Identify which results depend on assumptions or documents.</li><li>Explain one alternative, the trade-off, and the next action.</li></ol>"}]}},"health-underwriting-rhu-legacy":{"additions":{"module-1":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-1\">Compare plans across low, routine and high use</a> · <a href=\"#check-1\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-2":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-2\">Read stop-loss and test claim volatility</a> · <a href=\"#check-2\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-3":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-3\">Make a verified enrollment timeline</a> · <a href=\"#check-3\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-4":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-4\">Audit an explanation of benefits and plan appeal</a> · <a href=\"#check-4\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-5":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-5\">Coordinate Medicare choices with employer coverage</a> · <a href=\"#check-5\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","capstone":"<h4>Second case</h4><p><a href=\"#advanced-case\">The employee’s midyear plan change</a></p>","start":"<h4>Study sequence</h4><p>In each module, begin with the foundation lessons, then work through the added decision lesson and application check. Write your reasoning before opening the answer. Finish both cases and the course review. The module checks are self-scored.</p><p><a href=\"#capstone\">Start the capstones</a> · <a href=\"https://www.nabiptraining.org/nabip-training/curriculum/nabip-rebc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Issuer’s curriculum</a> · <a href=\"https://www.healthcare.gov/coverage-outside-open-enrollment/special-enrollment-period/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HealthCare.gov coverage transition and enrollment rules</a></p>"},"byModule":{"module-1":[{"id":"deep-1","title":"Compare plans across low, routine and high use","html":"<h3>Compare plans across low, routine and high use</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Calculate annual premiums and estimate eligible member share for likely care. Review negotiated rates, deductible, copays, coinsurance and out-of-pocket caps. Verify prescription tiers, clinicians and facilities directly. Model separate low, routine and high-use scenarios, including costs that do not count toward a cap.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>Plan A premium is $4,800 annually with $1,400 expected eligible cost share. Plan B premium is $6,000 with $600 expected share. Routine total is $6,200 versus $6,600. At in-network caps of $6,500 and $3,800, high-use totals are $11,300 and $9,800. The client may reasonably prefer one based on risk capacity and network.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-1\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-1\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-1","title":"Module 1 application check","html":"<h3>Module 1 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>How much less is Plan B in the high-use illustration?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$400</li><li>$800</li><li>$2,700</li><li>$1,500</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>D. $1,500. Compare $11,300 and $9,800.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-1\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-2":[{"id":"deep-2","title":"Read stop-loss and test claim volatility","html":"<h3>Read stop-loss and test claim volatility</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Distinguish specific stop-loss, which applies to defined claims for a covered individual, from aggregate stop-loss, which addresses qualifying claims above a group threshold. Confirm exclusions, lasers, timing and whether reimbursements arrive after amounts must be paid. Compare surplus sharing and run-out under actual contract terms.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>Assume a single member has $150,000 of qualifying claims and the specific attachment is $80,000 with no other limit. The potential reimbursement is $70,000. The employer may still need liquidity to pay claims before reimbursement.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-2\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-2\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-2","title":"Module 2 application check","html":"<h3>Module 2 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>If a member-specific threshold is $110,000, what is potential reimbursement under the same simplified assumptions?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$80,000</li><li>$110,000</li><li>$40,000</li><li>$70,000</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>C. $40,000. Subtract $110,000 from $150,000.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-2\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-3":[{"id":"deep-3","title":"Make a verified enrollment timeline","html":"<h3>Make a verified enrollment timeline</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Write the coverage end date, eligibility evidence, application date, effective date and first premium deadline for each option. For Marketplace assistance use the correct household and annual income estimates and official guidance. Compare continuation, spouse coverage and qualifying Marketplace options. Confirm medication access before ending old coverage.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>An employee loses group coverage June 30. Continuation may preserve access under its terms, while a Marketplace replacement may change network and effective date. Ask for the written termination notice and verify qualification, application timing and coverage effective date before deciding which route limits gaps.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-3\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-3\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-3","title":"Module 3 application check","html":"<h3>Module 3 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>What is the strongest reason to request written confirmation before dropping existing coverage?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Continuation and Marketplace coverage use identical start rules</li><li>The replacement’s eligibility, premium and effective date must be confirmed to avoid an uncovered interval.</li><li>A broker’s quoted premium legally starts coverage</li><li>Benefits always begin immediately</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>B. The replacement’s eligibility, premium and effective date must be confirmed to avoid an uncovered interval.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-3\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-4":[{"id":"deep-4","title":"Audit an explanation of benefits and plan appeal","html":"<h3>Audit an explanation of benefits and plan appeal</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Trace the claim number, provider, service date and network status. Compare billed amount, allowed amount, excluded charges, negotiated adjustment and member responsibility. Read the denial code and obtain the applicable plan provisions and appeal instructions. Use only the information needed to resolve the case.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>A bill is $2,000 and the in-network allowed amount is $1,200. Assume $300 remaining deductible, 20% coinsurance and that the procedure qualifies. Member share is $300 plus 20% of $900, or $480. The plan pays $720 and the $800 billed-to-allowed adjustment is separate on the stated facts.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-4\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-4\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-4","title":"Module 4 application check","html":"<h3>Module 4 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>What is member share if the deductible had already been met?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$240</li><li>$400</li><li>$480</li><li>$800</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>A. $240. Apply 20% to the $1,200 allowed amount before caps.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-4\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-5":[{"id":"deep-5","title":"Coordinate Medicare choices with employer coverage","html":"<h3>Coordinate Medicare choices with employer coverage</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Verify when coverage can start, employer plan status, who pays first and whether prescription coverage is creditable. Compare Original Medicare and Medicare Advantage by provider access, drug coverage, cost-sharing and geographic use. Evaluate Medigap timing and underwriting where applicable. Supplemental cash policies have separate triggers and do not substitute for these decisions.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>A worker retiring in four months uses an out-of-state specialist and a costly prescription. Verify current employer coverage, build the transition dates, check the specialist and medication under each option and compare travel and costs. Avoid assuming a representative can guarantee future network participation.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-5\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-5\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-5","title":"Module 5 application check","html":"<h3>Module 5 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>Which comparison best addresses these facts?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Monthly premium alone</li><li>Brand of the insurer alone</li><li>A hospital indemnity payout alone</li><li>Enrollment and effective dates, providers, prescriptions, coordination and costs under the actual alternatives.</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>D. Enrollment and effective dates, providers, prescriptions, coordination and costs under the actual alternatives.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-5\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"capstone":[{"id":"advanced-case","title":"The employee’s midyear plan change","html":"<h3>The employee’s midyear plan change</h3><h4>Case facts</h4><p>An employee needs a specialty drug and two specialists. Plan A costs $390 per month, with $2,000 in expected eligible member costs and a $7,000 cap. Plan B costs $560, with $300 expected member costs and a $4,000 cap. The employee is losing current coverage in six weeks.</p><h4>Submit a written recommendation</h4><p>Compare routine and high-use budgets. Build a transition checklist for dates, network, medication access and applicable enrollment options.</p><details><summary>Reveal the worked case and compare your reasoning</summary><p>Routine annual total is $6,680 versus $7,020. At the in-network caps totals are $11,680 versus $10,720. Compare drug tier, authorization, facility and doctors, and documented replacement effective date. Plan A saves $340 in routine use and Plan B saves $960 at the stated caps.</p></details><h4>Review your recommendation</h4><ol><li>Reconcile each calculation to source facts.</li><li>Identify which results depend on assumptions or documents.</li><li>Explain one alternative, the trade-off, and the next action.</li></ol>"}]}},"cebs-us-prep":{"additions":{"module-1":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-1\">Model benefits design at employee and sponsor levels</a> · <a href=\"#check-1\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-2":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-2\">Build an eligibility, notice and claims control</a> · <a href=\"#check-2\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-3":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-3\">Compare benefits vendors and document governance</a> · <a href=\"#check-3\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-4":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-4\">Design and cost an employer contribution</a> · <a href=\"#check-4\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","module-5":"<h4>Practice the decisions in this module</h4><p><a href=\"#deep-5\">Assess retirement plan investments, fees and distributions</a> · <a href=\"#check-5\">Application check with answer explanation</a></p>","capstone":"<h4>Second case</h4><p><a href=\"#advanced-case\">The second-year contract comparison</a></p>","start":"<h4>Study sequence</h4><p>In each module, begin with the foundation lessons, then work through the added decision lesson and application check. Write your reasoning before opening the answer. Finish both cases and the course review. The module checks are self-scored.</p><p><a href=\"#capstone\">Start the capstones</a> · <a href=\"https://www.ifebp.org/cebs/home/courses-and-materials/courses-united-states\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Issuer’s curriculum</a> · <a href=\"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/about-ebsa/our-activities/resource-center/faqs/retirement-plans-and-erisa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Department of Labor retirement plan and ERISA guidance</a></p>"},"byModule":{"module-1":[{"id":"deep-1","title":"Model benefits design at employee and sponsor levels","html":"<h3>Model benefits design at employee and sponsor levels</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Start with eligibility and tier enrollment from the census. Calculate employer and employee contributions by tier. Compare benefit changes on the same enrollment base and model shifts in employee behavior. Assess employer cash needs, participant affordability and higher-cost scenarios under alternative funding arrangements.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>Forty employee-only enrollees have $600 premiums with a 75% employer share. Twenty family enrollees have $1,600 premiums with a 60% share. Employer monthly cost is $18,000 plus $19,200, or $37,200. Annual cost is $446,400 before fees or changes.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-1\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-1\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-1","title":"Module 1 application check","html":"<h3>Module 1 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>If five employee-only enrollees move to family coverage, what is the employer’s monthly cost?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$37,200</li><li>$39,450</li><li>$42,000</li><li>$39,750</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>D. $39,750. Each shift adds $960 − $450 = $510, so five add $2,550.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-1\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-2":[{"id":"deep-2","title":"Build an eligibility, notice and claims control","html":"<h3>Build an eligibility, notice and claims control</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Map the sponsor, plan administrator, carrier and payroll responsibilities for hires, terminations and changes. Confirm the plan’s eligibility date instead of guessing from payroll. Read the summary plan description, actual plan text, and governing rules. Document notices and delivery. Escalate health or disability denials using the applicable claims and appeals procedure.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>An employee shows as terminated in the carrier file but is still active under the plan’s eligibility facts. Preserve source records, confirm the correct effective date, correct the vendor feed and reconcile payroll. Identify claims denied during the error and track reprocessing. Verify notice obligations independently.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-2\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-2\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-2","title":"Module 2 application check","html":"<h3>Module 2 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>What would prove the correction is complete?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A payroll change with no carrier confirmation</li><li>A new insurance card with no date verification</li><li>Corrected effective dates in both systems, verified claim reprocessing where applicable, and documented participant communication.</li><li>A verbal agreement to check next year</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>C. Corrected effective dates in both systems, verified claim reprocessing where applicable, and documented participant communication.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-2\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-3":[{"id":"deep-3","title":"Compare benefits vendors and document governance","html":"<h3>Compare benefits vendors and document governance</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Define the required service, plans covered, cost basis and evaluation criteria before opening proposals. Normalize administrative fees, performance guarantees, implementation cost, data rights and conflicts. Keep meeting notes linking the decision to evidence. Put claim reporting, eligibility reconciliation and participant communication on a calendar.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>Vendor A quotes $34 per member per month for 200 members and $12,000 implementation. Vendor B quotes $29 per member per month plus $22,000 implementation. Year one costs are $93,600 and $91,600. Compare scope and second-year economics before choosing.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-3\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-3\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-3","title":"Module 3 application check","html":"<h3>Module 3 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>If there are 250 members and the fees are unchanged, how much less does Vendor B cost in year one?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$15,000</li><li>$5,000</li><li>$2,000</li><li>$10,000</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>B. $5,000. The $5 monthly difference saves $15,000 annually, offset by $10,000 more implementation.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-3\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-4":[{"id":"deep-4","title":"Design and cost an employer contribution","html":"<h3>Design and cost an employer contribution</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>Compare defined contribution and defined benefit designs against staffing, owner goals, budget, eligible compensation, participation and administrative effort. Model matches and employer nonelective contributions separately. Review the governing document, vesting and employer responsibilities. Verify legal limits and testing with the administrator before launch.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>A stylized match pays 100% of the first 3% and 50% of the next 2% of pay. A worker with $80,000 eligible pay defers 5%. The match is $2,400 plus $800, or $3,200 before plan constraints. A worker deferring less may earn a smaller match.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-4\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-4\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-4","title":"Module 4 application check","html":"<h3>Module 4 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>Under the same formula, what is the match if the worker defers 4%?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$2,800</li><li>$2,400</li><li>$3,200</li><li>$4,000</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>A. $2,800. Match the first $2,400 at 100% and the next $800 at 50%.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-4\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"module-5":[{"id":"deep-5","title":"Assess retirement plan investments, fees and distributions","html":"<h3>Assess retirement plan investments, fees and distributions</h3><h4>Learn the decision process</h4><p>List participant costs and sponsor costs, distinguishing fixed, asset-based and transactional fees. Compare services, asset allocation, performance relative to an appropriate benchmark and investment restrictions. For distributions, confirm participant eligibility, money source, withholding and rollover options. For defined benefit funding, use the actuary’s valuation assumptions and basis.</p><h4>Follow the worked example</h4><p>A plan has $10 million in assets and 200 participants. Vendor A charges $200 per participant plus 0.12% of assets, or $52,000. Vendor B charges $45,000 fixed plus 0.05%, or $50,000. If assets rise while headcount stays flat, the two fee models change at different rates.</p><h4>Review the evidence before advising</h4><p>On a blank page, list the stated facts, your assumptions, the outcome you calculated and the document that could change your answer. Then try the module check.</p><p><a href=\"#check-5\">Continue to the module check</a> · <a href=\"#module-5\">Return to the module</a></p>"},{"id":"check-5","title":"Module 5 application check","html":"<h3>Module 5 application check</h3><p>Work without notes. Choose the best answer, then explain the assumptions before opening the solution.</p><p><strong>At $15 million in assets, what are the annual modeled fees?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Vendor A $52,000 and Vendor B $50,000</li><li>Vendor A $55,000 and Vendor B $60,000</li><li>Both $57,500</li><li>Vendor A $58,000 and Vendor B $52,500</li></ol><details><summary>Check the answer and reasoning</summary><p>D. Vendor A $58,000 and Vendor B $52,500. Compare services and conflicts as well as cost.</p></details><p><a href=\"#deep-5\">Review the worked lesson</a></p>"}],"capstone":[{"id":"advanced-case","title":"The second-year contract comparison","html":"<h3>The second-year contract comparison</h3><h4>Case facts</h4><p>A health vendor charges $31 per member per month plus $18,000 implementation in year one. Another charges $37 monthly with no implementation fee. There are 250 enrolled employees, and headcount holds flat for two years. A retirement vendor also proposes an added 0.10% fee on $24 million in assets.</p><h4>Submit a written recommendation</h4><p>Compare health costs in years one and two, calculate the retirement cost increase and draft a committee memo with verification steps.</p><details><summary>Reveal the worked case and compare your reasoning</summary><p>Health vendor A costs $111,000 in year one and $93,000 in year two. Vendor B costs $111,000 each year. Two-year totals are $204,000 and $222,000. Retirement cost rises $24,000 annually. Normalize service scope and guarantees and document conflicts, data ownership and renewal rights.</p></details><h4>Review your recommendation</h4><ol><li>Reconcile each calculation to source facts.</li><li>Identify which results depend on assumptions or documents.</li><li>Explain one alternative, the trade-off, and the next action.</li></ol>"}]}}}'::jsonb;
  course_key text;
  patch jsonb;
  existing jsonb;
  revised jsonb;
  additions jsonb;
  children jsonb;
  expected_new integer;
BEGIN
  FOR course_key, patch IN SELECT key, value FROM jsonb_each(changes) LOOP
    SELECT lessons INTO STRICT existing FROM public.tac_academy_courses WHERE slug=course_key FOR UPDATE;
    additions := patch->'additions';
    children := patch->'byModule';
    IF EXISTS (
      SELECT 1 FROM jsonb_object_keys(additions) AS t(id)
      WHERE (SELECT count(*) FROM jsonb_array_elements(existing) x WHERE x->>'id'=t.id) <> 1
    ) THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Unexpected Academy lesson structure: %', course_key;
    END IF;
    SELECT sum(jsonb_array_length(value)) INTO expected_new FROM jsonb_each(children);
    SELECT jsonb_agg(entry ORDER BY seq, slot) INTO revised FROM (
      SELECT n seq, 0 slot,
        CASE WHEN additions ? (lesson->>'id') AND strpos(lesson->>'html', additions->>(lesson->>'id'))=0
          THEN jsonb_set(lesson, '{html}', to_jsonb((lesson->>'html')||(additions->>(lesson->>'id'))))
          ELSE lesson END AS entry
      FROM jsonb_array_elements(existing) WITH ORDINALITY AS old(lesson,n)
      WHERE lesson->>'id' !~ '^(deep-[0-9]+|check-[0-9]+|advanced-case)$'
      UNION ALL
      SELECT old.n, child.slot, child.item
      FROM jsonb_array_elements(existing) WITH ORDINALITY AS old(lesson,n)
      CROSS JOIN LATERAL jsonb_array_elements(COALESCE(children->(old.lesson->>'id'), '[]'::jsonb))
        WITH ORDINALITY child(item,slot)
    ) AS ordered;
    IF jsonb_array_length(revised) <>
      (SELECT count(*) FROM jsonb_array_elements(existing) x WHERE x->>'id' !~ '^(deep-[0-9]+|check-[0-9]+|advanced-case)$') + expected_new
      OR EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM jsonb_array_elements(revised) x GROUP BY x->>'id' HAVING count(*)<>1)
    THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Academy expansion count or ID mismatch: %', course_key;
    END IF;
    UPDATE public.tac_academy_courses SET lessons=revised WHERE slug=course_key;
  END LOOP;
END;
$$;
DO $$
DECLARE
  labs jsonb := '{"ricp-prep":[{"id":"decision-lab-1","title":"Income flooring and survivor income","html":"<h3>Income flooring and survivor income</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Build a table with one row per essential expense and one per dependable income source. Mark variable portfolio returns outside the floor. A single-life pension may pay more while both spouses are alive but leave a larger survivor gap. Compare a joint election with separately funded survivor protection and liquidity.</p><h4>Worked scenario</h4><p>A household needs $70,000 of essentials. Social Security provides $36,000 and a proposed pension $26,000, leaving $8,000 of essentials exposed. After the first death assume essentials fall to $54,000, Social Security falls to $24,000 and a joint pension pays $13,000. The survivor floor gap is $17,000.</p><h4>Practice without notes</h4><p>Recalculate if an alternative election pays $20,000 while both spouses live and $20,000 to the survivor, with other figures unchanged.</p><details><summary>Reveal the calculation and reasoning</summary><p>The first gap becomes $14,000 and the survivor gap $10,000. This trades $6,000 more annual portfolio need initially for $7,000 less survivor need. Compare actual plan quotes and whether longevity or liquidity would change the preference.</p></details>"},{"id":"decision-lab-2","title":"Risk capacity and reserve replenishment","html":"<h3>Risk capacity and reserve replenishment</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Assign cash to planned cash flows by date and keep emergency reserves distinct from bridge funding. Decide how a planned draw will be replenished after a decline. Write a rule that names the trigger, amount and funding source. Compare what happens when a low-return period lasts longer than the cash reserve.</p><h4>Worked scenario</h4><p>A retiree owns $100,000 cash. $35,000 is reserved for emergencies. Retirement income gaps are $42,000 in year one and $24,000 in year two. Remaining bridge cash is $65,000 versus $66,000 needed, before interest. The reserve is one thousand short even though total cash looks ample.</p><h4>Practice without notes</h4><p>If the emergency reserve increases to $45,000 and year-one gap decreases to $40,000, quantify bridge shortfall.</p><details><summary>Reveal the calculation and reasoning</summary><p>$100,000 − $45,000 = $55,000 available versus $64,000 needed, a $9,000 shortfall. Compare prospective savings, reduced discretionary spending or planned portfolio sales.</p></details>"}],"clu-prep":[{"id":"decision-lab-1","title":"Read competing policy designs year by year","html":"<h3>Read competing policy designs year by year</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Create columns for each policy’s owner, face amount, term, premium, guaranteed duration, projected cash value and surrender value. For permanent coverage, request an illustration at the proposed premium and alternative assumptions. Plans based on supplemental riders need separate trigger and benefit checks. Match the policy to the client’s actual period of need.</p><h4>Worked scenario</h4><p>Policy A charges $240 monthly for 20-year level term. Policy B uses $720 monthly for a permanent policy, with a guarantee only under stated premium conditions. Both start at $750,000 of face value in the exercise. At $950 monthly protection and savings capacity, A leaves $710 per month and B leaves $230. This arithmetic alone does not value future coverage.</p><h4>Practice without notes</h4><p>Calculate the cumulative premium difference over five years if both payments hold. Identify what you would compare at year 20.</p><details><summary>Reveal the calculation and reasoning</summary><p>$28,800. Determine which policy can still provide coverage, its cost, conversion or renewal rights, guarantee funding, the client’s needs then, and what happened to the cash-flow difference.</p></details>"},{"id":"decision-lab-2","title":"Separate operating risk, creditor risk and ownership transfer","html":"<h3>Separate operating risk, creditor risk and ownership transfer</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Before deciding on life coverage for an owner, draw three lines: cash the business needs to continue, obligations that mature at death and the amount the buyer owes the estate. Assign a specific owner and beneficiary to each insurance purpose. Speak with counsel before changing corporate ownership or using business funds for the personal obligation.</p><h4>Worked scenario</h4><p>An owner’s death could create a $250,000 operating shortfall and trigger a $1 million purchase. A creditor has a $150,000 priority assignment against a $900,000 policy payable to the buyer. Under the exercise assumptions the buyer receives $750,000 and needs $250,000 more. The operating shortfall is separate.</p><h4>Practice without notes</h4><p>If collateral debt is repaid and the release becomes effective before death, what is the new modeled buyout gap?</p><details><summary>Reveal the calculation and reasoning</summary><p>$100,000, if the buyer receives the full $900,000 and enters no other transaction. The separate $250,000 operating gap remains.</p></details>"}],"cic-prep":[{"id":"decision-lab-1","title":"Compare a claim with a fictional endorsement","html":"<h3>Compare a claim with a fictional endorsement</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>A coverage decision should name the underlying grant, the specific modified wording and the facts that draw the claim into or out of coverage. A sublimit caps a defined category under the contract. A deductible can apply before or after a limit according to the actual wording. A certificate does not establish either number.</p><h4>Worked scenario</h4><p>Fictional endorsement for training: “Equipment temporarily at a job site is eligible for direct physical loss caused by collision. The most payable after applying the $2,000 deductible is $30,000 for all equipment per occurrence.” A covered collision damages equipment at a site for $38,000. Deduct $2,000 then cap at $30,000. A theft at that site requires separate review.</p><h4>Practice without notes</h4><p>A collision causes $24,000 to one item and $12,000 to a second item in the same occurrence. What payment does this wording permit, assuming both items qualify and one deductible applies?</p><details><summary>Reveal the calculation and reasoning</summary><p>$30,000. Aggregate $36,000, subtract $2,000 and apply the occurrence cap. The stated limit applies to the occurrence rather than separately per item.</p></details>"},{"id":"decision-lab-2","title":"Separate actual cash value from replacement cost","html":"<h3>Separate actual cash value from replacement cost</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Review what the policy promises to pay, and when. An illustrative actual cash value estimate can subtract stated depreciation. Replacement cost treatment can require actual repair or replacement and timely proof. Check valuation, agreed value or insurance-to-value requirements, sublimits and exclusions before relying on either number.</p><h4>Worked scenario</h4><p>Comparable replacement costs $96,000, depreciation is $30,000 and the deductible is $5,000. Assume the form pays ACV initially and any recoverable depreciation after eligible replacement. Initial payment is $61,000, with up to $30,000 additional subject to the form. The person who cannot fund a replacement may face a real liquidity gap.</p><h4>Practice without notes</h4><p>If replacement ultimately costs $84,000 and the policy caps total payout at actual eligible replacement cost less deductible in this illustration, how much additional payment is available?</p><details><summary>Reveal the calculation and reasoning</summary><p>$18,000. Full allowable total is $79,000, minus $61,000 already paid. Verify the real form before applying this method.</p></details>"}],"health-underwriting-rhu-legacy":[{"id":"decision-lab-1","title":"Build a prescription access and annual cost comparison","html":"<h3>Build a prescription access and annual cost comparison</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Verify strength, dosage, pharmacy and specialty or mail-order rules. Check formularies for restrictions such as prior authorization and step therapy. Estimate client cost under each plan’s actual cost-sharing order. Check whether services count toward deductible and out-of-pocket limits. Write down verification date and source.</p><h4>Worked scenario</h4><p>Plan A premium costs $4,200 yearly and the assumed medication share is $220 monthly. Plan B costs $5,400 yearly and the medication share is $75 monthly. Before other costs, annual totals are $6,840 and $6,300. The cheaper premium loses in this example if both coverage assumptions hold.</p><h4>Practice without notes</h4><p>If eligibility for Plan B’s drug terms is confirmed only for three months and the remaining months’ cost is unknown, what must the recommendation say?</p><details><summary>Reveal the calculation and reasoning</summary><p>The $6,300 total is a scenario, not a verified annual forecast. Obtain coverage terms and model interruption, substitutions and higher costs before presenting the result.</p></details>"},{"id":"decision-lab-2","title":"Choose group plan comparison metrics","html":"<h3>Choose group plan comparison metrics</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Use a consistent enrollment population, contribution split, scope, time period and claim assumption. For self-funded proposals check reserves, specific and aggregate stop-loss, contract timing and exclusions. A monthly quote may hide claim volatility or one-time fees. Include employee disruption alongside dollars.</p><h4>Worked scenario</h4><p>An insured plan costs $510,000 annually. A proposal estimates $390,000 claims, $48,000 administration and $55,000 stop-loss premiums. Base estimate is $493,000. At 10% higher claims before reimbursements, modeled cost is $532,000. Ask who funds the claims while reimbursement is pending.</p><h4>Practice without notes</h4><p>How much higher can claims go before the base estimate reaches the insured premium, assuming all else constant?</p><details><summary>Reveal the calculation and reasoning</summary><p>$17,000, or about 4.36% of expected claims. Actual equivalence also depends on coverage, timing and stop-loss.</p></details>"}],"cebs-us-prep":[{"id":"decision-lab-1","title":"Reconcile a retirement plan contribution file","html":"<h3>Reconcile a retirement plan contribution file</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Compare eligible employee, pay definition, deferral election and payroll record against the plan document and vendor posting. Build exception categories such as missing participants, eligibility timing and incorrect match. Compare amounts across pay periods. Review correction paths with plan counsel or administrator before changing participant balances.</p><h4>Worked scenario</h4><p>A worker earns $5,000 eligible pay this month and elects 6%, implying $300 deferral. Assume the plan matches 50% up to 6% and the worker is eligible. Vendor shows $240 deferral and $120 match. Total modeled discrepancies are $60 employee and $30 employer. Identify whether elections, payroll or posting caused the issue.</p><h4>Practice without notes</h4><p>If vendor instead shows $300 deferral and $120 match, what exception is still open?</p><details><summary>Reveal the calculation and reasoning</summary><p>A $30 match discrepancy. Reconcile the applicable plan match, pay period and payroll posting with the administrator.</p></details>"},{"id":"decision-lab-2","title":"Write and defend a plan committee decision","html":"<h3>Write and defend a plan committee decision</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Identify fiduciary responsibility, alternatives, vendor conflicts and supporting evidence. Review reasonable fees in relation to services, investment options and participant outcomes. Note which facts are missing and specify who will obtain them. Set a review date and document what triggers earlier follow-up.</p><h4>Worked scenario</h4><p>Two providers quote $45,000 fixed plus 0.10% of assets, and $60,000 flat. At $12 million the first costs $57,000 and the second $60,000. Document participant service, investment lineups, fee allocation and what happens as the plan grows. Cost alone does not identify equivalent providers.</p><h4>Practice without notes</h4><p>At what asset balance do the fees become equal, and what could still make you pick one over the other?</p><details><summary>Reveal the calculation and reasoning</summary><p>$15 million, because the first provider’s variable fee then reaches $15,000. Evaluate service quality, equivalent scope, investment options and transition risk.</p></details>"}]}'::jsonb;
  target text;
  collection jsonb;
  existing jsonb;
  nav text;
BEGIN
  FOR target, collection IN SELECT key,value FROM jsonb_each(labs) LOOP
    SELECT lessons INTO STRICT existing FROM public.tac_academy_courses WHERE slug=target FOR UPDATE;
    IF (SELECT count(*) FROM jsonb_array_elements(existing) x WHERE x->>'id'='workbook') <> 1
      THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Academy workbook missing: %', target;
    END IF;
    SELECT '<h4>Additional decision labs</h4><ul>' || string_agg('<li><a href="#'||(l->>'id')||'">'||(l->>'title')||'</a></li>','') || '</ul>'
      INTO nav FROM jsonb_array_elements(collection) l;
    UPDATE public.tac_academy_courses SET lessons=(
      SELECT jsonb_agg(CASE WHEN x->>'id'='workbook' AND strpos(x->>'html',nav)=0
        THEN jsonb_set(x,'{html}',to_jsonb((x->>'html')||nav)) ELSE x END ORDER BY n)
      FROM jsonb_array_elements(existing) WITH ORDINALITY old(x,n)
      WHERE x->>'id' NOT LIKE 'decision-lab-%'
    ) || collection WHERE slug=target;
  END LOOP;
END;
$$;
COMMIT;
